internal object AppletBuildGuide {
    const val HTML = """<p><strong>Required for creation and changes:</strong> read the <a href="https://bridgit.cc/learn/builder/applet-practices.md">shared applet practices</a> and <a href="https://bridgit.cc/learn/builder/practices.json">catalog</a>. Use runtime defaults. Record <code>ui.practices</code>, review new reusable lessons, and justify scoped exceptions before validation. Inspect <code>practice_compliance</code>; structural validity does not prove behavioral tests.</p>
<p><strong>Agents: start with the <a href="https://bridgit.cc/learn/builder/quickstart.md">compact applet quickstart</a></strong>: verify this device, understand the owner’s goal, search current templates and discover capabilities before building.</p>
<p><strong>Changing an existing applet?</strong> Read <a href="https://bridgit.cc/learn/builder/modifying-applets.md">Modifying applets and templates</a> first: preserve the personal copy, read its development snapshot, and record intent and checks before updating. Detach when repurposing; do not overwrite a personal applet with a template.</p>
<p>Start with one useful outcome: what should the phone notice, decide or show, and how will the owner know it worked? An applet is one package file the owner approves once. The phone builds and runs every part itself and draws the card in Bridgit's own style. You choose what to sense, how to decide and what to do; you don't design the screen. Most applets need no native build or APK installation.</p>
<section class="card"><h3>1. Start from the closest recipe</h3>
<p>Read the <a href="https://bridgit.cc/learn/start.md">short builder starter</a>, or ask a paired phone <code>GET /v1/discover?q=&lt;the idea&gt;</code>. Need one capability's details? Search the <a href="https://bridgit.cc/learn/catalog-index.json">compact capability index</a> and follow only that one. Every recipe has a ready package: <a href="https://bridgit.cc/recipes/pet-monitor/">pet activity</a>, <a href="https://bridgit.cc/recipes/room-change-watcher/">room changes</a>, <a href="https://bridgit.cc/recipes/situation-counter/">situation counter</a>, <a href="https://bridgit.cc/recipes/receipt-scanner/">receipt OCR</a>, <a href="https://bridgit.cc/recipes/voice-notebook/">voice notes</a>, <a href="https://bridgit.cc/recipes/daily-briefing-panel/">daily briefing</a> and <a href="https://bridgit.cc/recipes/external-device-dashboard/">device dashboard</a>, each at <code>/recipes/&lt;id&gt;/applet.json</code>.</p>
<p>After the owner connects you, check <code>GET /v1/health</code> and <code>GET /v1/capabilities</code>. Use a capability only when it shows <code>agent_allowed</code>, <code>enabled</code> and <code>ready</code>; otherwise name the one switch the owner should turn on. A public example grants no access.</p></section>
<p>Before choosing capabilities, look up the task in the <a href="https://bridgit.cc/learn/builder/practices.json">shared applet practices catalog</a>. Each practice lists evidence and verification status.</p>
<section class="card"><h3>Capture skills for any applet</h3>
<p>For a person taking a photo or video, choose a preset below. Set <code>viewfinder:true</code>, include a short <code>instructions</code> sentence about framing, and let the person review, retake or delete the capture.</p>
<ul><li><b>Photo:</b> Use <code>POST /v1/camera/snapshot</code> with <code>countdown_seconds:0</code>. Choose front for a portrait or rear for a subject. The shutter takes the photo immediately.</li>
<li><b>Group photo:</b> Add <code>group_photo:true</code>. With microphone access, the photographer can tap Listen for GO and say “go” once everyone is ready. Keep the manual shutter available.</li>
<li><b>Video:</b> Use <code>POST /v1/camera/video</code> with <code>countdown_seconds:0</code> and a duration suited to the subject. Ask the person to hold steady or pan slowly. Leave audio off unless sound is part of the request.</li></ul></section>
<section class="card"><h3>2. Choose the parts</h3>
<p>Query <code>GET /v1/services</code> (MCP <code>services</code>) for general external services any applet can use. Select GLM 5.3 Flash with <code>model: "glm"</code> in a judgment, watch or flow <code>ask_model</code> step. The catalog names required grants, call formats and budget configuration; discovery makes no paid call and grants no access.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://bridgit.cc/learn/builder/applet-packages.md">package reference</a> has every field and a worked example. Pick from:</p>
<ul><li><b>Watches:</b> camera or sensor checks on the phone, a free change check, Clef for photos and sound, Jev for text, a tally that counts answers and rules such as "fewer than one meal by 18:00". Camera <code>model:none</code> is free change detection, not recognition. Set a paid-call cap (<code>max_paid_per_hour</code>, and the applet's <code>budget</code>). The free change check may reuse an older answer until its freshness limit; a reused result is not new model evidence.</li>
<li><b>Flows:</b> a trigger (button, interval, charging, a place, a watch match or a rule) and steps from a fixed menu: take a photo, ask GLM 5.3 Flash, Jev or Clef, ask the owner and wait, notify, speak, ring, alert a contact, record a row. Later steps use earlier results; any step can be conditional and says what happens if it fails. Every run is logged.</li>
<li><b>Tables:</b> rows kept on the phone (receipts, notes, meals), with photos. The owner can see and delete them.</li>
<li><b>A schedule</b> for actions at set times (a manifest awaiting owner approval is not active), and a <b>budget</b> that caps paid model calls.</li>
<li><b>Network devices</b> the owner enrolled: listing an enrolled device does not probe it or prove it online.</li></ul>
<p>An installed applet keeps running while your controller is offline; your own controller's timers and storage stop when it stops, so keep on the phone whatever must keep going.</p>
<p>Read <a href="https://bridgit.cc/learn/decisions.md">decision contracts, costs and limits</a> before adding GLM Flash, Jev or Clef: they are remote, paid and capped. Unavailable cameras, ambiguous audio and absent pets are unknown, not negative findings.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h3>3. Show it with card pieces</h3>
<p>The card is a list of pieces the phone draws: header, status, metrics, photo gallery, video, table, timeline, people, devices, an editable draft and buttons. Each reads from a source: a watch's tally, a table, the flow's runs, kept photos or one read-only call. Loading, empty, stale, paused and error states are automatic. Buttons refresh, pause, run the flow, record a row or make one API call as the applet. <code>GET /v1/samples?id=pieces-status</code> is a complete card you can post on its own.</p>
<p>Custom HTML is the escape hatch for what pieces can't draw (start from <code>GET /v1/samples?id=html-connection-test</code>): it runs offline in a sandbox, reaches the phone only through <code>Bridgit.act</code> and must keep its own loading, empty, stale and error states. A card is pieces or HTML, not both.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h3>4. Install, verify and hand over</h3>
<p><code>POST /v1/applets/validate</code> with <code>{"url": …}</code> or <code>{"package": …}</code> and <code>config</code> returns the owner's review, <code>warnings</code> and the complete file. <code>POST /v1/applets/install</code> asks the owner; <code>applet.installed</code> arrives in <code>GET /v1/events</code>. Your agent needs the Install applets permission.</p>
<p>Test with a known example and the owner's agreement. Report what really ran: the flow's run log, the watch's checks, the owner's answer. A delivered notification is not proof the owner saw it. Tell the owner how to pause or remove the applet (the card's Pause button, or Applets › Installed applets), and what stays after removal. Stopping does not erase every record: interaction answers and logs keep their own lifetimes; tables and kept photos go with the applet.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h3>Optional: add native integration</h3>
<p>Change the Android app only when packages can't meet the goal or the owner specifically wants native integration. Follow the repository's current instructions and tested native patterns, document ownership, grants, stopping and retention, and add endpoint descriptions and focused tests. Ordinary applets don't need this step.</p></section>

<section class="card"><h3>Modify an installed applet</h3>
<p>The owner’s Modify applet handoff contains only the installed instance ID and the <a href="https://bridgit.cc/learn/builder/modifying-applets.md">control-agent modification instructions</a>. Read the exact development snapshot and follow those instructions before proposing changes.</p></section>
